@meta
id: 12-06
title: Where else does this id appear
chapter: 12
type: lesson
skills: search-star, search-next
par: 24
minutes: 4
vscode: select the id, Ctrl+F, Enter, then the previous-match button
open: payments.log

@brief
The last line of this log is the one that paged somebody:

    ERROR charge.duplicate gateway_ref=ch_7T21QK order=ord_8891

That order was charged twice. The first, legitimate charge is somewhere
above, and the reconciliation job needs to be told to leave it alone.

Find the earlier line that mentions the same order and append ` skip=true`
to the end of it. Nothing else in the log changes.

You are starting at the bottom of the file with the id already in front
of you. Neither typing it out nor scrolling up looking for it is the
move.

@keys
| `*` | search forward for the word under the cursor |
| `#` | search backward for the word under the cursor |
| `G` `$` | last line, end of line |
| `A` | append at the end of the line |

@hints
Get the cursor onto the order id on the last line. There is a key that
searches for the word under the cursor, and it has a backwards twin.
--
`*` goes forward, `#` goes backward. Here you want backward, because the
earlier charge is above you. Both count `ord_8891` as one word —
underscores and digits are part of a word for this purpose.
--
`G` then `$` leaves the cursor on the last character of the id, which is
inside the word, which is all `#` needs. Then `A` and the text.

@solution
G  $  #  A skip=true<Esc>  :wq<Enter>

Nineteen keystrokes and you never typed `ord_8891`, which matters more
than it sounds: ids are exactly the strings people mistype.

`#` is `*` pointed backwards, and in a log that is usually the direction
you want. Logs are read from the failure upwards — something went wrong
at 09:19, so what happened to this id before that? `#` answers that
question one keystroke at a time, and `n` keeps going in the same
direction because `#` set the search direction to backwards.

Both keys match whole words only. If you want the loose version — the one
that would match `ord_8891` inside a longer string — that is `g*` and
`g#`. You will want them for partial identifiers and file paths, and you
will want the strict ones for everything else.

